# Memory exam: four pairs of sessions on one computer each.
# Session a runs first and gives facts or creates data; session b (adjacent, same computer) only has the computer's files
# Session a's solution includes a remember line and a notes file (replay skips remember). How session a stores the fact is its own
# choice (the remember action or a file), so only session b checks that the fact survived.

== m1a-zen-facts
computer: mem1
prompt: Project facts you must keep: the project folder is zen-42, the service port is 48213, and every report file name ends in .rpt.
| You will not see this conversation again, so keep these facts in your notes.
| Now create zen-42/README.txt containing the service port.
check: read zen-42/README.txt => contains 48213
solution: write zen-42/README.txt
| service port 48213
solution: remember Project folder zen-42, service port 48213, report names end with .rpt
solution: write notes/memory.md
| Project folder: zen-42
| Service port: 48213
| Report file names end with .rpt
solution: done Created zen-42/README.txt with the port.

== m1b-zen-report
computer: mem1
prompt: Create the status report for the project: a file named status.rpt inside the project folder, containing the service port.
| Then tell me the port.
check: read zen-42/status.rpt => contains 48213
check: answer => contains 48213
solution: read notes/memory.md
solution: write zen-42/status.rpt
| service port 48213
solution: done 48213

== m2a-ploverline-facts
computer: mem2
prompt: Facts to keep, because you will not see this conversation again: the service is called Ploverline, its health-check port is 61407, and its config lives in ops/cfg.
| Create ops/cfg/service.txt containing the service name.
check: read ops/cfg/service.txt => contains Ploverline
solution: write ops/cfg/service.txt
| Ploverline
solution: remember Service Ploverline, health-check port 61407, config folder ops/cfg
solution: write notes/memory.md
| Service: Ploverline
| Health-check port: 61407
| Config folder: ops/cfg
solution: done Created ops/cfg/service.txt.

== m2b-ploverline-health
computer: mem2
prompt: Add the health-check file for the service: ops/cfg/health.txt containing a single line with the health-check port.
| Then tell me that port.
check: read ops/cfg/health.txt => contains 61407
check: answer => contains 61407
solution: read notes/memory.md
solution: write ops/cfg/health.txt
| 61407
solution: done 61407

== m3a-open-records-count
computer: mem3
setup: run iridium --json sql records.idb "CREATE TABLE records (id INTEGER, status TEXT)"
setup: run iridium --json sql records.idb "INSERT INTO records VALUES (1, 'open'), (2, 'closed'), (3, 'open'), (4, 'open'), (5, 'closed'), (6, 'open'), (7, 'closed'), (8, 'open'), (9, 'closed')"
prompt: Count the records in the records table of records.idb whose status is open, and tell me the number.
| You will not see this conversation again, so keep the result in your notes.
check: answer => contains 5
solution: run iridium sql records.idb "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n FROM records WHERE status = 'open'"
solution: remember Open records in records.idb: 5
solution: write notes/memory.md
| Open records in records.idb: 5
solution: done 5

== m3b-open-records-report
computer: mem3
setup: run iridium --json sql records.idb "DROP TABLE records"
prompt: The records table in records.idb has been removed from the database.
| Write report.txt containing the number of open records found earlier, and tell me that number.
check: read report.txt => contains 5
check: answer => contains 5
solution: read notes/memory.md
solution: write report.txt
| 5
solution: done 5

== m4a-blob-prefix
computer: mem4
setup: write data/blob.txt
| payload 4471 for memory test
prompt: Compute the sha256 of data/blob.txt and find its first 8 hex characters.
| You will not see this conversation again, so keep that prefix in your notes.
check: answer => contains 42915764
solution: run sphinx --json hash sha256 data/blob.txt
solution: remember Hash prefix of the original data/blob.txt: 42915764
solution: write notes/memory.md
| Hash prefix of the original data/blob.txt: 42915764
solution: done 42915764

== m4b-blob-prefix-report
computer: mem4
setup: write scrub.edit
| goto 1 1
| delete 999
| insert replaced content
| save
setup: run calliope --json edit scrub.edit data/blob.txt
prompt: The file data/blob.txt has been changed since the last session.
| Write report.txt containing the 8-character sha256 prefix that the original data/blob.txt had, and tell me that prefix.
check: read report.txt => contains 42915764
check: answer => contains 42915764
solution: read notes/memory.md
solution: write report.txt
| 42915764
solution: done 42915764
